  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 Days...\n\nAs the low pressure system moves to the east, the precipitation\nwill move out with it. Scattered snow showers will linger across\nour CWA through mid afternoon but minimal impacts are expected\nwith these. The winds will continue increasing into the afternoon\nhours before quickly dropping after sunset. Winds in the 850-700mb\nlayer over SE MT will increase as the surface low moves into ND\nwith speeds around 50kts near KMLS and up to 60kts near KBHK.\nThese winds aloft combined with clearing clouds will allow for \nstrong winds to mix down to the surface through sunset today. \n\nWeak ridging will move into the region overnight tonight and\ncontinue through the day Thursday. This will not bring much \nchange in temperatures, however much lighter winds and minimal \nchance of precip are forecast Thursday. These conditions will also\nbe favorable for the development of fog both Thursday and Friday \nmorning. The areal extent and density of the fog may be a little \nless than previously forecast due to the lower than forecast \nprecip amounts that fell as a result of the exiting storm. \nRegardless, prepare for a potentially foggy morning impacting the \nAM commute. \n\nA few notable details have changed in regards to the next system\nforecast to begin impacting our CWA Friday. Starting with the big\npicture, the overall trough has changed shape and location\nslightly. The main energy moving ashore off the Pacific will still\nhave the same general path moving due east into the four corners\nregion. The low diving out of the north now is being depicted as a\nlittle stronger which could aid in general ascent by the time it \narrives early Saturday. Although the main trough will be a little \ntoo far south, surface cyclogenesis is forecast around KCYS \nbefore moving directly northeast. This will put much of our CWA in\na good spot for enhanced lift in association with the TWOWAL on \nthe backside of the surface low. Additionally this will result in \nsome good NNE winds across the foothills which could produce a \nperiod of upslope snow enhancement Saturday. This is fairly new \namongst the model runs, however there is decent agreement between \nthe models for this outcome to occur. The main limiting factor \nhere will be moisture content as we will not see any meaningful \nmoisture advection through the event at all. It does appear that \nnearly all of the CWA has a good chance at picking up \naccumulating snowfall though with KBIL potentially even seeing \ntheir first 1\".\n\nThe increased strength of the secondary low moving in out of AB\nwill help keep slight snow showers around the area through the\nweekend. Although the surface low and main trough will be exiting\nour area Saturday evening, the smaller upper level low will linger\nover Montana through the rest of the weekend. Even less moisture\nin our area at this time will mean that there will be minimal\naccumulation with any lingering showers. \n\nModels continue to show an active pattern going into \nThanksgiving week, however there is not much certainty regrading \nany details. Be sure to check the forecast frequently if you have \ntravel plans for the holiday. WMR",
